      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/8b1286adb93e98a6d7b3ece98100c9ad6a51b39549a849ebfa382f2056d055bc.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇒Q(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/1e308a124ef6367ff9c877098b76671ed0a40320edf634298a652f3ab187b40a.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{p}$ or ${T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/9bf7e5e7c6ddd8ce5faa8e47529454865cfc41e6525de57a811c7dcf38e46154.png)\n\nAs a result, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14179858/assets/b48fdc8f6de1d9cb599b0c9a149c3146f969717301a28d81cde083c3f36e80f3.png)",
